        name: "Ghumot", family: "Percussion", note: "A hollow, bulging earthen vessel with openings at both ends. The larger mouth carries a stretched membrane.", video: "YouTube search: Ghumot & Shamel Demonstration",
        detailsText: `State / region of origin: Goa; especially associated with Goan folk performance traditions such as Zagor and with festival and devotional music.
Associated community: Goan folk musicians and communities participating in Zagor and other local performance traditions. It is not limited to one religious community.
Materials used: A hollow, bulging earthen vessel with openings at both ends. The larger mouth carries a stretched membrane. Older examples used monitor-lizard skin; contemporary heritage-approved instruments use substitute materials because the monitor lizard is legally protected.
Who makes it: The clay body is shaped by a potter. The membrane and fastening are prepared by instrument makers or craftspeople. No single inventor is documented.
How it is played: The player holds the vessel against the body and strikes the membrane with the fingers or hands, producing a resonant bass and rhythmic tone.
Unique history / story: The ghumot is one of Goa’s distinctive indigenous percussion instruments. Its use is documented in folk song, dance-drama and festival settings. Historical writing on Goan performance also records that a Portuguese decree of 14 April 1736 prohibited the use of the ghumot and certain other Indian instruments in churches. The instrument nevertheless continued in local folk traditions. In 2019, Goa formally recognised the ghumot as a heritage folk instrument; the recognition also drew attention to replacing protected monitor-lizard skin with lawful alternatives. The decree is a documented historical episode; claims about the instrument’s exact age or first maker are not established.
Cultural significance: The ghumot links Goan folk music, village festivals and performance traditions across religious communities. It is heard in folk songs and dance-dramas, including Zagor, and in Shigmo and Ganesh celebrations. Its heritage recognition also makes it a useful case study in how cultural preservation can adapt instrument-making to wildlife-protection requirements.
Audio / Video: Ghumot & Shamel Demonstration
Research references: Goa instrument inventory (doctoral appendix) | Goa heritage status report`
